  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 257 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The dry and seasonable stretch continues through\ntonight. Highs will be in the 70s to low 80s and lows will be \nin the 50s and low 60s. An occluding low will track well to the \nnorth Tuesday into Wednesday, bringing several rounds of \nshowers. The showers look to begin during the day Tuesday and \ncontinue into Wednesday afternoon. Right now, the severe threat \nis relatively low. There does not look to be a well-defined \nboundary, instead several rounds of showers with plenty of \nclouds look to move through. There is very little time for solar\nheating and instability to form, and the airmass will be \nrelatively cool to start with. With the low tracking well to the\nnorth, the best synoptic forcing will be well to the north, \nthough there will be adequate wind shear. Overall, the region is\nin an SPC marginal risk which seems representative. While an \nisolated strong to severe storm is possible, that looks to be \nthe extent of it. The system does not look to provide much of a \nflooding threat either. GEFS/EPS/CMCE combined ensemble \nprobabilities of receiving more than an inch of rain are between\n10-30 percent. While there will be areas of convective \nenhancement, the storms will be quick-moving and there will be \ngaps between the showers. PWATs should rise into the 1.5-1.75 \ninch range but with the expected setup, that should not pose too\nmuch of a problem. While limited wildfire smoke may return for \nTuesday, its concentration will be significantly less than on \nSaturday and it will likely not have much of an effect on the \nconvection.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Thursday night through Sunday will feature \nquieter weather as we close out the week. Upper level trough \nwill pull away from the region and then surface high pressure \nwill ridge over our region Thursday night. A low pressure system\npasses well to our north Friday into Friday night, our region \nshould not have any precipitation associated with this system. \nThe weekend looks mainly dry with near normal temperatures. Next\nchance for precipitation will be Sunday night into Monday night\nas a frontal system and upper level trough crosses the region.",
